title: GUI
kind: overview
aliases: interface gui shell window
summary: The desktop interface: workspaces in tabs, one or two panels side by side, a command input, and status and task bars — all driven from the keyboard.

The GUI (`metafolder-gui`) is a window onto a running daemon: it loads and shows
repositories, edits metarecords, runs queries and reconciles, and walks the event
log — what `mf` does, in visual form. It connects to the daemon over HTTP and never
starts or stops it ([[Connection to the daemon]]).

From top to bottom, the window holds:

* the ''tab bar'' — one tab per [[workspace|Workspaces]];
* one or two ''panel slots'' side by side, each showing a panel of the workspace assigned to it ([[Panel slots and layout]], [[Panel types]]);
* the ''command input'', always there: a command name after `:`, a shell command after `!` ([[The command input]]);
* the ''task bar'', when something is in flight, and the ''status bar'' ([[Status bar and task bar]]).

Four principles shape it:

* ''Keyboard first'': every command can be reached from the keyboard, and every key can be rebound.
* ''Command driven'': everything the GUI does is a named command, typed in the command input or bound to a key; the menus and buttons invoke the same commands ([[Commands]]).
* ''Scriptable'': the GUI serves a local HTTP API, so that a script — through `mf gui` — can open panels and wait for the user's answer.
* ''Themeable'': its look is a CSS file read at startup.

Nothing of a session is kept: the GUI starts with one empty workspace each time.

!! How it is built

A Tauri v2 application: a Rust side and the system's web view (WebKit2GTK on
Linux). The Rust side owns every piece of state — workspaces, layout, workspace
variables, keybindings, the command registry — and the shell, written in Svelte,
only mirrors it through events; the scripting API and the window therefore always
agree. Panel types are plain HTML and JavaScript, with no build step, and run in
the shell's own page, each in its shadow root.

